{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794401532,"nonce":"fbRsvXYVkTdqXIwPoPPSg8WwpWQ=","title":"Signals: what the reactive helper covers and what it does not yet","message":"Asked for by the user on 2026-11-11: signals with fine-grained reactivity in the style of SolidJS, as a helper alongside Future and Stream.\n\nHaxe side, pure Haxe and so the same on every runtime (`haxe/hxn/`):\n\n- `Signal\u003cT\u003e`: `value`, `get`, `peek`, `set`, `update`, with an optional equality.\n- `Computed\u003cT\u003e`: Solid's memo. Named to match `ashui.reactive`, which also uses `Signal`, `Computed`, `root` and `onCleanup`.\n- `Reactive.effect`, `batch`, `untrack`, `onCleanup`, `root`.\n- `Reactive.follow`: a signal that follows a value a native library keeps current.\n\nThe algorithm (`hxn/reactive/Node.hx`) marks on write and computes on read: a write marks direct readers dirty and everything below them as needing a check, and queues effects; an effect or a read brings its own sources up to date first and recomputes only if one changed. That gives one computation however many paths lead to a value, no mix of old and new, and dependencies that follow the branch taken.\n\nRust side: `hxn_future::signal(initial)` gives a `Source` and a `Signal`. The host sees the latest value and a version; ten sets between two looks are one change to it. Dropping every source closes the signal; a source learns its reader is gone because `set` returns false.\n\nTested by 20 checks in `tests/counter/haxe/Signals.hx`, 5 Rust unit tests, and two followed signals in the fixture (a `Gauge` set from Haxe, and a `ramp` the library counts up by itself), on Ash in hybrid, jit and interp modes, Ash wasm, stock HashLink, hxcpp and Emscripten.\n\nNot covered:\n\n- Values are stored as `Dynamic`, so an `Int` or `Float` signal is boxed on HashLink and hxcpp. ashui has typed variants (`SignalI32` and so on) for that reason.\n- A graph belongs to one thread, and nothing checks that it is used from one.\n- Following holds an event-loop promise until the library closes the value or the owning root is disposed, so a followed signal outside a root keeps a program alive while its source lives.\n- Only Haxe follows Rust. A Haxe signal cannot yet drive a Rust value except through a method the binding calls.\n- No stores, resources, context, transitions or `on(...)` from SolidJS; only the core primitives.\n- Effects run synchronously when a signal is set outside a batch. SolidJS defers user effects until after rendering; there is no render phase here to defer to.\n- The generator does not emit signals yet.","files":null}]}